{
  "schemaVersion": 3,
  "id": "ink",
  "name": "Ink",
  "tagline": "Drift · no beat: a warm chord whose inner voices move, one at a time.",
  "description": "No pulse. A warm minor seventh held by four voices; every minute or so one inner voice crossfades to a new note, so the harmony keeps moving without a chord ever arriving. Over a session the colour moves between Aeolian and Dorian, one note at a time. A drone, harmonics far above, pitched air.",
  "use": {
    "name": "Write",
    "for": "Writing: drafting and revising prose or code comments, holding sentences in mind.",
    "why": "Writing holds word order in mind, which separate changing pieces disrupt and continuous sound does not (rule 2); legato did not disrupt where staccato did. Ink gives more motion than Still, all of it continuous: voices change by slow crossfade, never as a new attack, and colour shifts move one note per step (rules 6 and 22: slow, expected change goes unnoticed and costs little). Evidence: strong for the mechanism on verbal memory; writing itself is studied only by extrapolation (the one paper on music while writing cannot anchor a rule)."
  },
